  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "D6v8bS5Yot4h5/aw12Nuu3JsgMU=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:34.410Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Dimpact is a cooperative of more than 40 municipalities that jointly develops and procures open-source software. It built PodiumD and co-built KISS, so it is a producer as well as a buyer: the members pool requirements and engineering, not only purchasing power.\n\nThe research identifies the structure rather than the product \u2014 a federated buyer, the same shape as the Danish bank IT cooperatives noted elsewhere in the corpus. The consequence is arithmetic. One relationship reaches 40+ municipalities, so the cost of a single integration is amortised across all of them; equally, a rejection removes 40+ prospects at once.\n\nThat is why the research calls it probably the highest-leverage single conversation in the Benelux. It also implies a particular motion. Cooperatives decide collectively and slowly, and what they adopt they then maintain, so the qualifying question is whether a component can be handed over as shared infrastructure rather than whether one municipality has budget this year.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**1 \u00b7 parallel track**\n- Dimpact (NL)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> COOPERATIVE OF 40+ MUNICIPALITIES that jointly develops and procures open-source software. Built PodiumD; co-built KISS. \u2014 A federated buyer, structurally the same shape as the Danish bank IT cooperatives. One relationship reaches 40+ municipalities. Probably the highest-leverage single conversation in the Benelux.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:32:34.410710Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:32:34.410726Z"
    }
  },
